\section{Writing a formula for RSiena}{

Each effect is written as its RSiena short name, with the covariate or
network it reads as its argument: \code{ego(sex)} is \code{egoX(sex)},
\code{same(sex)} is \code{sameX(sex)}, \code{diff(age)} is \code{absDiffX(age)}, a layer
such as \code{primary} is \code{X(primary)}, and \code{trans} is \code{transTrip}.
\link{terms_mrqap} gives the effect for each term. A formula written in
RSiena's short names, such as \code{friends ~ recip + transTrip + inPop + egoX(sex)}, is read as one written for RSiena, and an effect that has no
equivalent in the other models, such as \code{inPop}, is kept as it is.
\code{density} is always added, as the outdegree effect that every SAOM of a
network has. Every other evaluation effect is in the model only if the
formula names it: \code{recip}, which RSiena includes by default, is left out
where the formula does not name it, and a message says so.
A random intercept, a fixed effect, a reporter term, \code{global()}, and
(for now) an interaction are dropped. Add an interaction to the result
with \code{RSiena::includeInteraction()}.
}

\section{The data and the dependent variables}{

With \code{.data}, the network is coerced with \code{manynet::as_siena()}.
The left-hand side of the formula names the dependent network, which
must be a layer of the network, or \code{.} for its focal layer. A list of
formulas names several dependent networks, one for each formula.
Only these layers are dependent variables: every other layer becomes a
dyadic covariate, and every node attribute a covariate, constant or
changing across the waves. A dependent behaviour is not yet written here;
for one, build the effects with \code{RSiena::getEffects()}.
Each effect is checked against the effects that RSiena has for the data,
and a name that it does not have aborts with the nearest name.
The model is then fitted with \code{RSiena::siena()} of the data and the
effects, as in the last example. \code{siena()} writes an output file in the
working directory, named after its data argument, unless
\code{control_out = RSiena::set_output_saom(outputName = )} names another.
}

\section{What each model makes of each term}{

\code{summary()} of the result has one row for each term of the formula, and
fits nothing. Its \code{status} is what the model makes of the term:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fits"}: the model fits the term as it is written;
\item \code{"adapted"}: the term is rewritten as its equivalent in the model, in \code{as};
\item \code{"interpreted"}: the model has a term of the same name that it defines
differently, as \code{reason} says;
\item \code{"warns"}: the model fits the term, but with a warning, as \code{reason} says;
\item \code{"drops"}: the model cannot fit the term, so it is left out, and the rest
is fitted;
\item \code{"refuses"}: the model aborts, as \code{reason} says.
}

An MRQAP warns for a term that reads the outcome's own ties, such as
\code{recip}, and refuses \code{tie()}, \code{indeg()}, and \code{outdeg()} of the outcome
layer. It drops an offset, such as goldfish's \code{offset(recip, coef = 0)},
since it estimates every coefficient; ergm keeps an ergm one. For an MRQAP, \code{summary()} with \code{.data} also builds each term and names
its \code{columns}, as \code{\link[=estimate_mrqap]{estimate_mrqap()}} names them.
}

\section{Reading the model a formula was written for}{

The model a formula was written for is read, in order, from \code{input},
from the class of the formula, such as \code{"ergm_formula"}, or from the
names in the formula:
\itemize{
\item \code{"ergm"} where a term is named only in ergm, such as \code{edges},
\code{mutual}, or \code{nodematch("x")};
\item \code{"saom"} where an effect is named only in RSiena, such as \code{transTrip},
\code{inPop}, or \code{egoX(sex)};
\item \code{"dynam"} where a term is named only in goldfish, such as \code{inertia} or
\code{trans};
\item \code{"mrqap"} otherwise.
}

A \code{sienaEffects} object of \code{{RSiena}} is read as the formula of the
evaluation effects it includes for its first dependent network.

The model that was read is reported. Where a formula mixes ergm's names
with goldfish's, it is read as an ergm formula, and goldfish's names in it
are read as they are in goldfish.
The result of each function carries its model as its class, so that
\code{specify_ergm(specify_mrqap(f))} reads its input without a guess.
}

